  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nDry west to northwest flow aloft will bring dry and mild conditions \nto the surface through early next week. A weak boundary will sag \ninto the area later tonight into Sunday as low pressure moves east \nalong the Canadian border. Other than a little moisture in the 500 \nto 300 mb layer, dry air below will keep any precipitation from \nforming. Given the mild low levels, expect highs in the 50s and 60s \nSunday and Monday, warmer on Monday.\n\nAnother weak surface cool front sags south Monday night into \nTuesday, but high temperatures will still climb well into the 50s. \nOnce again, the wave to the north responsible for this weak front \nlacks moisture, so precipitation is not expected.\n\nA sharp ridge of high pressure will develop over the western \nU.S. and allow for a northerly upper level jet to dive south \nonto the Plains Tuesday night into Wednesday. This will likely \nbring a quick shot of light precipitation with only minor \nimpacts anticipated. The latest EC, Canadian and GFS ensemble \noutput only hinting at a 5-10 percent chance for more than a \ntenth of an inch of rain.\n\nThe sharp ridge of high pressure aloft strengthens through the week, \nkeeping northerly flow in place through the end of the week with \nnear to below normal temperatures expected. This ridge is then \nexpected to spread across the Plains neat weekend which would \nsupport very mild, above normal temperatures. Precipitation during \nthis time is not expected.",
